# ChainQuest — Blockchain-Integrated Unity Game

ChainQuest is a Unity 3D game prototype that integrates blockchain technology into a traditional game inventory system.

The project demonstrates how Non-Fungible Tokens (NFTs) can represent in-game items and how a Unity application can verify item ownership using an Ethereum smart contract.

## Project Objectives

- Develop a playable Unity game prototype with collectible items.
- Deploy an ERC-721 NFT smart contract on the Ethereum Sepolia test network.
- Integrate Unity with the blockchain using Nethereum.
- Retrieve NFT metadata and verify ownership.
- Allow players to collect items only when their wallet owns the corresponding NFT.
- Demonstrate how transferring an NFT affects access to its in-game representation.

## Technologies Used

| Technology | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Unity | Game development and user interface |
| C# | Gameplay logic and blockchain integration |
| Solidity | NFT smart contract development |
| Ethereum Sepolia | Blockchain test network |
| ERC-721 | NFT token standard |
| Nethereum | Ethereum integration for .NET / Unity |
| Alchemy | Ethereum RPC provider |
| MetaMask | Wallet management and transaction signing |
| Remix IDE | Smart contract deployment and interaction |
| Git & GitHub | Version control |

## System Architecture

The application communicates with the Ethereum Sepolia network through Nethereum and an Alchemy RPC endpoint.

```text
Unity Game
    |
    v
BlockchainManager.cs
    |
    v
Nethereum (Web3)
    |
    v
Alchemy RPC
    |
    v
Ethereum Sepolia
    |
    v
ChainQuestNFT Smart Contract
```

The game reads blockchain data and uses it to determine whether an item can be collected.

The Unity application does not require MetaMask to remain open during normal gameplay because the implemented functionality uses read-only blockchain queries.

## Smart Contract

The ChainQuestNFT smart contract is deployed on the Ethereum Sepolia test network.

**Contract address:**

`0x255Dc7Cd7cF27C22b41997e4245c41149f859eE1`

**Network:** Ethereum Sepolia

**Chain ID:** 11155111

The contract supports NFT ownership verification, NFT transfers, and retrieval of item metadata.

Important functions include:

- `ownerOf(tokenId)` — retrieves the current owner of an NFT.
- `getItemDetails(tokenId)` — retrieves the item's name, rarity, and description.
- `safeTransferFrom(from, to, tokenId)` — transfers an NFT between wallets.

## In-Game NFT Items

The prototype currently uses two NFT-backed items.

| Token ID | Item | Rarity |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Ancient Sword | Rare |
| 2 | Magic Crystal | Epic |

Each item has an associated token ID and metadata stored in the smart contract.

When a player attempts to collect an item, the game checks the corresponding NFT's owner against the configured player wallet address.

If ownership is verified, the item is added to the in-game inventory.

If ownership does not match, collection is denied.

## Implemented Features

- Player movement and interaction with collectible objects.
- Two collectible NFT-backed items.
- Connection to Ethereum Sepolia.
- Smart contract validation during initialization.
- Retrieval of NFT ownership and metadata.
- Ownership-based item collection.
- Inventory interface displaying item information.
- Blockchain connection status indicator.
- Retry connection functionality for connection errors.
- Visual representation of collectible items in the Unity scene.

## Functional Testing

The main experiment tested whether transferring an NFT between wallets changes the availability of its corresponding in-game item.

| Test Scenario | Expected Result | Actual Result |
|---|---|---|
| Wallet A owns NFT #1 | Ancient Sword can be collected | Passed |
| NFT #1 transferred to Wallet B | Wallet A cannot collect Ancient Sword | Passed |
| Wallet A still owns NFT #2 | Magic Crystal can be collected | Passed |
| NFT #1 transferred back to Wallet A | Ancient Sword becomes collectible again | Passed |
| Item collected successfully | Item appears in inventory and disappears from scene | Passed |

The experiment confirmed that access to the corresponding in-game item changes according to NFT ownership recorded on the blockchain.

## Local Inventory vs Blockchain-Verified Inventory

| Local Inventory | Blockchain-Verified Inventory |
|---|---|
| Ownership is managed by the game | NFT ownership is recorded on the blockchain |
| Item data can be stored locally | NFT metadata can be retrieved from a smart contract |
| Item transfers require game-specific logic | NFTs can be transferred using blockchain transactions |
| Inventory checks can work offline | Ownership verification requires blockchain access |

ChainQuest demonstrates a hybrid approach: Unity manages gameplay and the displayed inventory, while the blockchain provides an external source of ownership information.

## Setup

### Requirements

- Unity with the project's required packages
- Internet connection
- Ethereum Sepolia RPC endpoint
- Nethereum dependencies included in the Unity project

### Configuration

1. Clone the repository.
2. Open the project in Unity.
3. Create a configuration file at:

   `Assets/StreamingAssets/blockchain-config.json`

4. Add the following configuration:

```json
{
  "rpcUrl": "https://eth-sepolia.g.alchemy.com/v2/YOUR_API_KEY"
}
```

5. Ensure the BlockchainManager references the deployed contract and the intended player wallet address.
6. Open `Assets/Scenes/Main.unity`.
7. Enter Play Mode.

**Security note:** The RPC configuration file is excluded from version control. Do not commit private API keys, wallet private keys, or recovery phrases.

## Limitations

- The prototype operates on Ethereum Sepolia rather than Ethereum Mainnet.
- NFT ownership verification requires an internet connection and a functioning RPC endpoint.
- The game uses a configured player wallet address rather than an interactive wallet-login system.
- Blockchain transactions are performed externally through wallet tools.
- The in-game inventory is a gameplay representation of verified NFT ownership, not an independent on-chain inventory.
- The prototype focuses on demonstrating blockchain integration rather than providing a complete commercial game.

## Future Improvements

- Interactive wallet connection.
- NFT minting directly from Unity.
- In-game NFT transfers.
- Additional NFT-backed items and quests.
- More comprehensive error-handling and performance testing.
- Expanded gameplay and inventory functionality.

## Conclusion

ChainQuest demonstrates the integration of an ERC-721 smart contract with a Unity game.

Functional testing confirmed that transferring an NFT to another wallet prevents the original wallet from collecting the corresponding in-game item. Transferring the NFT back restores access.

The project illustrates how blockchain-based ownership verification can be incorporated into a conventional game inventory system.
